. cite46†C Shipped Optimization Guidelines L57:   13. cite47†D Ablation Results L58:     1. cite48†Sampled kernels. L59:     2. cite49†Observations. L60:   14. cite50†E NCU Profiling Case Studies L61:     1. cite51†Why sub-kernel and E2E speedups diverge. L62:     2. cite52†L1-007: Register pressure reduction. L63:     3. cite53†L1-012: Algorithmic rewrite. L64:     4. cite54†L2-018: Operator fusion with algebraic simplification. L65:   15. cite55†F Complete KernelBench Results L66:     1. cite56†F.1 Fallback Analysis L67:     2. cite57†F.2 Multi-Kernel Composition Gap: Full Case Listing L68:     3. cite58†F.3 L3 Architecture Breakdown L69:   16. cite59†G Generated Kernel Examples L70:     1. cite60†G.1 L1 Kernel 012: Diagonal Matrix Multiply (88.63$\\times$) L71:       1. cite61†Before (Inductor via cuBLAS). L72:       2. cite62†After (KernelOPT synthesized Triton kernel). L73:       3. cite63†Optimization pass. L74:     2. cite64†G.2 L2 Kernel 018: Algebraic Simplification (33.11$\\times$) L75:     3. cite65†G.3 L1 Kernel 007: Matmul Autotune Optimization (2.89$\\times$) L76:   17. cite66†H Tutorial Kernels L77:   18. cite67†I Gate 3 Correctness Verification: $\\rho$ Distribution Analysis L78:     1. cite68†I.1 Quantitative Summary (rejected candidates only) L79:     2. cite69†I.2 Key Observations L80:   19. cite70†J Hardware and Software Configuration L81: cite71†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L82: \nL83: arXiv:2609.30059v2 [cs.DC] 06 Oct 2026\nL84: # KernelOPT: Dispatch-Aware Agentic Search\nL85: for GPU Kernel Optimization\nL86: Aheli Poddar ^{†}^{†}thanks: Equal contribution (joint first authors).^{†}^{†}thanks: Corresponding author.^{†}^{†}thanks: The first four authors completed this work during an internship with the PyTorch team at Red Hat.\nL87: Affiliation: Red Hat Email: ahpoddar@redhat.com    Sanskar Prasad^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1 ^{3}^{3}footnotemark: 3 Affiliation: Red Hat Email: sanspras@redhat.com    Arindam Samanta^{3}^{3}footnotemark: 3 Affiliation: Red Hat Email: arsamant@redhat.com    Subha Chakraborty^{3}^{3}footnotemark: 3 Affiliation: Red Hat Email: subhchak@redhat.com    Vishal Goyal Affiliation: Red Hat Email: visgoyal@redhat.com    Rohit Singh Rathaur Affiliation: Red Hat Email: rrathaur@redhat.com\nL88: ###### Abstract\nL89: Deep learning inference and training performance depends critically on GPU kernel efficiency. Modern compilers such as PyTorch Inductor automatically generate GPU kernels from high-level model code, but frequently underperform expert-written implementations by wide margins.\nL90: Recent LLM-assisted kernel optimizers can close this gap for standalone kernels, yet treat compiled models as black boxes, generally optimizing individual standalone kernels without respecting the compiler’s structural decisions or verifying the model end-to-end. We present KernelOPT, a multi-agent system that treats compiled models as structured artifacts. It preserves vendor library calls (cuBLAS, cuDNN) and exclusively targets generated Triton sub-kernels using five profiling-guided LLM agents.\nL91: A four-gate verification cascade applies static validation, multi-seed correctness checking, model-level float64-fallback verification, and performance gating ($\\gamma{=}1.03$) to filter candidates and verify the re-stitched model end-to-end. When candidates fail verification, the system preserves the compiler baseline. The system accepts PyTorch nn.Module s, standalone Triton kernels, and Helion kernels.\nL92: Evaluated on 250 KernelBench problems (100 Level 1, 100 Level 2 and 50 Level 3) on NVIDIA H200, KernelOPT achieves geometric mean speedups over torch.compile of 1.40$\\times$ (L1), 1.15$\\times$ (L2), and 1.07$\\times$ (L3) across all kernels, including fallback cases.\nL93: Optimized-only geomeans (excluding cases where verification gates preserve the compiler baseline) are substantially higher: 2.54$\\times$ (L1: 36/100), 1.84$\\times$ (L2: 23/100), and 1.37$\\times$ (L3: 11/50), reflecting where the optimizer achieves meaningful leverage.\nL94: ## 1 Introduction\nL95: Efficient GPU kernels are the foundation of performant deep learning. PyTorch Inductor (cite72†Ansel et al., 2024 ), the default torch.compile backend, delivers a 2.27$\\times$ geometric mean inference speedup over eager mode by lowering operations to Triton (cite73†Tillet et al., 2019 ). However, the generated code leaves substantial performance on the table.\nL212: #### Gate 3: Model-level correctness ($V_{\\text{model}}$).\nL213: \nL214: After optimization, the re-stitched model undergoes end-to-end verification with stricter tolerances ($\\epsilon_{r}{=}\\epsilon_{a}{=}10^{-4}$). When strict comparison fails (common for TF32 tensor-core paths), a float64 reference disambiguates: with $r_{f64}$ the baseline’s FP64 output, $d_{\\text{ref}}=\\|r_{f32}-r_{f64}\\|_{\\infty}$ and $d_{\\text{opt}}=\\|o_{f32}-r_{f64}\\|_{\\infty}$, the error ratio\nL215:  | $$\\rho=d_{\\text{opt}}\\,/\\,d_{\\text{ref}}\\,,\\qquad\\text{pass if }\\rho\\leq 10$$  |  | (4)\nL216: (when $d_{\\text{ref}}\\geq 10^{-8}$; a scale-relative check handles exact-reference operations like argmax, and an absolute bound handles fused-kernel precision anchoring – full formulas in Appendix cite42†B ). Correct FP32 kernels are expected at $\\rho\\in[1,5]$ (cite104†Blanchard et al., 2020 ); TF32 fallback or algorithmic bugs push $\\rho$ orders of magnitude higher (cite105†Stosic, 2021 ; cite106†PyTorch Contributors, 2024 ), making $\\rho_{\\max}{=}10$ a conservative firewall.\nL217: We log $\\rho$ only for rejected candidates: all 167 logged rejections fall at $\\rho\\geq 1{,}569$ ($157\\times$ above threshold); the accepted-side distribution is not yet instrumented (future work, §cite33†7 ; full statistics in Appendix cite67†I ).\nL218: Weight extraction handles Inductor-externalized parameters: we intercept argument flattening via $\\texttt{torch.compile}(m,\\text{backend}=f_{\\text{capture}})$ to recover real model state, and replace only data inputs with seed-consistent values (full mechanism in Appendix cite42†B ).\nL219: #### Gate 4: Performance gate ($V_{\\text{perf}}$).\nL220: \nL221: With $\\tau_{M}$ the re-stitched model’s wall-clock time (subprocess-isolated do_bench, warmup=25 ms, rep=100 ms):\nL222: \nL223:  | $$V_{\\text{perf}}(k,k_{0})=\\mathbb{1}\\!\\left[\\tau_{M}(k_{0}\\!\\to\\!k)\\leq\\gamma\\cdot\\tau_{M}(k_{0})\\right],\\;\\gamma{=}1.03\\,.$$  |  | (5)\nL224: This catches cases where per-kernel speedup does not translate to model-level improvement (e.g. a Triton matmul with manual transpose strides forcing downstream implicit .contiguous() copies). Rejections get one targeted retry (up to 2 attempts) before the compiler baseline is preserved.\nL225: ### 4.5 Experience Memory\nL226: \nL227: Following AccelOpt (cite77†Zhang et al., 2026 ), KernelOPT maintains a bounded FIFO queue of capacity $Q$ storing experience items curated by asymmetric speedup thresholds:\nL228: \nL229:  | $$\\text{store}(e)=\\mathbb{1}[s(e)\\geq s_{+}]\\;\\vee\\;\\mathbb{1}[s(e)^{-1}\\geq s_{-}]\\,,$$  |  | (6)\nL230: \nL231: where $s(e)$ is the speedup and $s_{+}=1.05$, $s_{-}=1.20$. The asymmetry reflects that marginal improvements ($<$5%) are less informative than significant regressions ($>$20%).\nL232: We add two components: a cross-run strategy tracker flagging strategies with $<$30% success after $\\geq$3 attempts as AVOID, and benchmark-derived guidelines extracted post-hoc from the 250 completed runs as cold-start context for new targets (extracted after evaluation; do not affect Table cite83†1 ). Both serve as planner context, not hard constraints.\nL233: ## 5 Experimental Setup\nL234: All experiments run on a single NVIDIA H200 GPU (Hopper, CC 9.0, 132 SMs, 141 GB HBM3, 4.8 TB/s bandwidth) using Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic) accessed via Google Cloud Vertex AI.^{1}^{1} 1 API: cite107†https://cloud.google.com/products/model-garden/claude†cloud.google.com . KernelOPT’s LLM backend is pluggable and accepts any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (cite108†https://developers.openai.com/api/reference†developers.openai.com ).\nL235: The hyperparameters are fixed across all 250 problems with no per-level tuning: $T{=}5$ iterations, $N{=}4$ plans per iteration, $K{=}4$ executor retries, beam width $B{=}4$, performance gate margin $\\gamma{=}1.03$, memory queue capacity $Q{=}8$ with asymmetric thresholds $s_{+}{=}1.05$, $s_{-}{=}1.20$, and error ratio bound $\\rho_{\\max}{=}10$.\nL236: NCU profiling (ncu --set full, kernel replay) extracts SOL throughput, duration, registers, shared memory, occupancy, and all NCU rules ranked by estimated speedup; the top-3 rules are injected into the planner prompt. Per-call LLM timeout is 900 s.\nL237: #### Baseline selection.\nL238: Following the evaluation protocol of KernelBench (cite97†Ouyang et al., 2025 ), we use torch.compile (Inductor with max_autotune) as the primary baseline, isolating the marginal gain over the state-of-the-art deterministic compiler. We omit direct quantitative comparisons with other tools for two reasons. First, hardware sensitivity: our experiments run on an NVIDIA H200, whereas prior metrics are tied to H100 or AWS Trainium (cite77†Zhang et al., 2026 ) environments, rendering direct metric transfer invalid.\nL239: Second, architectural mismatch: prior systems optimize isolated kernels, whereas KernelOPT operates on the full compiled nn.Module, measuring dispatch overhead from re-stitching Triton kernels alongside preserved vendor libraries.\nL240: We evaluate on the full KernelBench benchmark (cite97†Ouyang et al., 2025 ): Level 1 (100 single-op kernels), Level 2 (100 composite operators), and Level 3 (50 model architectures), plus 10 Triton and 10 Helion tutorial kernels (270 total problems).\nL241: Performance is measured via do_bench (warmup=25 ms, rep=100 ms); per-candidate correctness uses the relaxed allclose($10^{-3}$) tolerance to avoid rejecting valid TF32 candidates during search, and the final end-to-end verification tightens to the KernelBench-standard $10^{-4}$. The total compute budget is ${\\sim}$1,100 GPU-hours on H200 (L1: ${\\sim}$145 h on 1 GPU; L2: ${\\sim}$210 h on 2 GPUs; L3: ${\\sim}$730 h on up to 8 GPUs in parallel, with individual models ranging from 6 min to 66 h).\nL242: Each problem is optimized for $T{=}5$ iterations with $N{=}4$ plans per iteration (divided across active chains, §cite18†4.3 ) and up to $K{=}4$ synthesis attempts per plan, yielding 20 total LLM-driven candidates per problem. The highest E2E speedup passing all four verification gates is reported; matched kernels (within the 3% noise margin) are deployed as-is, and fallback cases preserve the compiler baseline, so no reported result is a regression.\nL243: Due to stochastic LLM generation, results reflect the best candidate found within this search budget, not the theoretical ceiling.\nL244: ## 6 Results and Discussion\nL245: ### 6.1 Main Results\nL246: \nL247: Table cite83†1 breaks down all 250 KernelBench results by outcome category. We report both an all-problems geomean (including fallback cases, reflecting overall behavior on a diverse benchmark) and an optimized-only geomean (isolating successful optimizations).\nL248: \nL249:   * •\nL250: Optimized (36 L1 + 23 L2 + 11 L3 = 70): passes all four gates with speedup $>1.03\\times$ (optimized-only geomeans: $\\mathbf{2.54}\\times$/$\\mathbf{1.84}\\times$/$\\mathbf{1.37}\\times$). Gains are heavy-tailed (Panel B): a minority reach order-of-magnitude speedups via algorithmic rewrites or fusion, most fall in $1.03$–$1.5\\times$ from incremental register/occupancy/tiling gains diluted by Amdahl’s law.\nL251: \nL252:   * •\nL253: Matched (48 L1 + 43 L2 + 4 L3 = 95): within the 3% noise margin, or no optimization was attempted (deployed model is the compiler baseline). Both contribute ${\\approx}1.00\\times$ and are non-regressing by construction.\nL254: \nL255:   * •\nL256: \nL257: Fallback (16 L1 + 34 L2 + 35 L3 = 85): all candidates rejected or no Triton kernels generated; baseline preserved. Root causes (Panel D): library-dominant (75%), other operation types (11%), no Triton kernels (8%), terminal correctness failures (6%).\nL258: All-problems geomeans (including fallback, where each contributes $1.00\\times$): L1 1.40$\\times$, L2 1.15$\\times$, L3 1.07$\\times$ – this is why the optimized-only metric matters for gauging leverage where the optimizer actually acts.\nL259: A. Summary  |    B. Speedup distribution (70 opt.)\nL260: Level  | Total  | Opt.  | Matched  | Fall.  | Geo.  | Max  | Range  | L1  | L2  | L3  | Tot.\nL261: L1  | 100  | 36  | 48  | 16  | 1.40$\\times$  | 88.63$\\times$  | $\\geq$10$\\times$  | 4  | 1  | 0  | 5\nL262: L2  | 100  | 23  | 43  | 34  | 1.15$\\times$  | 33.11$\\times$  | $[5,10)$  | 6  | 4  | 0  | 10\nL263: L3  | 50  | 11  | 4  | 35  | 1.07$\\times$  | 4.12$\\times$  | $[2,5)$  | 7  | 0  | 2  | 9\nL264:  |  |  |  |  |  |  | $[1.5,2)$  | 2  | 2  | 1  | 5\nL265:  |  |  |  |  |  |  | $[1.1,1.5)$  | 8  | 8  | 4  | 20\nL266: All  | 250  | 70  | 95  | 85  | 1.23$\\times$  | 88.63$\\times$  | $[1.03,1.1)$  | 9  | 8  | 4  | 21\nL267: C. Dominant operation (70 optimized)  |     D. Fallback root causes (85 fallbacks)\nL268: --- | ---\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKernelOPT: Dispatch-Aware Agentic Searchfor GPU Kernel Optimization (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30059v2)\nciteturn11view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30059v2\",\"pattern\":\"Table 1\"}); Total lines: 1426\nL250: Optimized (36 L1 + 23 L2 + 11 L3 = 70): passes all four gates with speedup $>1.03\\times$ (optimized-only geomeans: $\\mathbf{2.54}\\times$/$\\mathbf{1.84}\\times$/$\\mathbf{1.37}\\times$). Gains are heavy-tailed (Panel B): a minority reach order-of-magnitude speedups via algorithmic rewrites or fusion, most fall in $1.03$–$1.5\\times$ from incremental register/occupancy/tiling gains diluted by Amdahl’s law.\nL251: \nL252:   * •\nL253: Matched (48 L1 + 43 L2 + 4 L3 = 95): within the 3% noise margin, or no optimization was attempted (deployed model is the compiler baseline). Both contribute ${\\approx}1.00\\times$ and are non-regressing by construction.\nL254: \nL255:   * •\nL256: \nL257: Fallback (16 L1 + 34 L2 + 35 L3 = 85): all candidates rejected or no Triton kernels generated; baseline preserved. Root causes (Panel D): library-dominant (75%), other operation types (11%), no Triton kernels (8%), terminal correctness failures (6%).\nL258: All-problems geomeans (including fallback, where each contributes $1.00\\times$): L1 1.40$\\times$, L2 1.15$\\times$, L3 1.07$\\times$ – this is why the optimized-only metric matters for gauging leverage where the optimizer actually acts.\nL259: A. Summary  |    B. Speedup distribution (70 opt.)\nL260: Level  | Total  | Opt.  | Matched  | Fall.  | Geo.  | Max  | Range  | L1  | L2  | L3  | Tot.\nL261: L1  | 100  | 36  | 48  | 16  | 1.40$\\times$  | 88.63$\\times$  | $\\geq$10$\\times$  | 4  | 1  | 0  | 5\nL262: L2  | 100  | 23  | 43  | 34  | 1.15$\\times$  | 33.11$\\times$  | $[5,10)$  | 6  | 4  | 0  | 10\nL263: L3  | 50  | 11  | 4  | 35  | 1.07$\\times$  | 4.12$\\times$  | $[2,5)$  | 7  | 0  | 2  | 9\nL264:  |  |  |  |  |  |  | $[1.5,2)$  | 2  | 2  | 1  | 5\nL265:  |  |  |  |  |  |  | $[1.1,1.5)$  | 8  | 8  | 4  | 20\nL266: All  | 250  | 70  | 95  | 85  | 1.23$\\times$  | 88.63$\\times$  | $[1.03,1.1)$  | 9  | 8  | 4  | 21\nL267: C. Dominant operation (70 optimized)  |     D. Fallback root causes (85 fallbacks)\nL268: --- | ---\nL269: Operation  | L1  | L2  | L3  | Tot.  | Root cause  | L1  | L2  | L3  | Tot.\nL270: Matmul / Linear  | 15  | 9  | 2  | 26  | GEMM-dominant (cuBLAS)  | 1  | 28  | 6  | 35\nL271: Conv / ConvTranspose  | 8  | 13  | 3  | 24  | Conv-dominant (cuDNN)  | 7  | 6  | 13  | 26\nL272: Reduction / Scan  | 9  | 1  | 2  | 12  | Other operation type  | 7  | 0  | 5  | 12\nL273: Norm / Pool / Other  | 4  | 0  | 4  | 8  | No Triton kernels (LSTM/GRU)  | 0  | 0  | 7  | 7\nL274:  |  |  |  |  | E2E correctness failure  | 1  | 0  | 4  | 5\nL275: Total  | 36  | 23  | 11  | 70  | Total  | 16  | 34  | 35  | 85\nL276: Table 1: Comprehensive KernelBench results vs. torch.compile. Panel A: Level summary; Matched includes both correct kernels within the 3% noise margin and cases where no optimization was attempted (deployed model is the compiler baseline). Panel B: Speedup distribution over the 70 optimized kernels. Panel C: Dominant operation breakdown (by the highest-speedup sub-kernel per problem; name-based classification).\nL277: Panel D: Fallback root causes, classified by dominant Triton kernel name among the 73 performance-regression fallbacks (cuBLAS/cuDNN dominance accounts for 75%), plus 7 no-Triton-kernel and 5 terminal-correctness-failure cases.\nL278: #### Speedup analysis.\nL279: \nL280: The speedup distribution is heavy-tailed (Panel B). Table cite109†8 (Appendix cite50†E ) illustrates how NCU bottleneck metrics (compute, memory, occupancy) drive the planner’s strategy selection across all three levels. The largest gains arise from two mechanisms.\nL281: Algorithmic optimization and fusion. The top five speedups span both mechanisms: L1 012 (diagonal matmul, $O(N^{3})\\to O(N^{2})$ row-scaling, 88.63$\\times$), L2 018 (operator fusion and algebraic simplification of a 5-operation chain, 33.11$\\times$), L1 015 (lower-triangular matmul, skip-zero iteration, 20.26$\\times$), L1 014 (upper-triangular solve, 14.04$\\times$), and L1 085 (batched diagonal, 11.38$\\times$).\nL282: Operator fusion. On L2 064, four fused pointwise operations on a $(256,4096)$ tensor eliminate intermediate memory round-trips (8.30$\\times$). L2 022 achieves 7.18$\\times$ via similar epilogue fusion.\nL283: \nL284: Beyond the 250 KernelBench problems, KernelOPT also improves 4/10 official Triton tutorial kernels (best $1.70\\times$) and 5/10 Helion tutorials (best $1.08\\times$); see Appendix cite66†H .\nL285: ### 6.2 Speedup Baselines: vs. Compile and vs. Eager\nL286: \nL287: To provide context for real-world deployment, we report speedups against both torch.compile and eager PyTorch:\nL288: Level  | vs compile (all)  | vs compile (opt-only)  | vs eager (all)  | vs eager (opt-only)\nL289: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL290: L1 (100)  | 1.40$\\times$  | 2.54$\\times$  | 1.72$\\times$  | 2.64$\\times$\nL291: L2 (100)  | 1.15$\\times$  | 1.84$\\times$  | 1.79$\\times$  | 2.82$\\times$\nL292: L3 (50)  | 1.07$\\times$  | 1.37$\\times$  | 1.56$\\times$  | 1.69$\\times$\nL293: Table 2: Geometric mean speedups vs. torch.compile and eager mode, computed directly from the logged Eager/Compile/KernelOPT do_bench timings for each problem. “All” includes fallback cases: for these, the deployed model is torch.compile (vs. compile $=1.0\\times$ by construction; vs. eager $=$ compile’s own measured speedup over eager for that problem). “Opt-only” reports geomean over the 70 successfully optimized kernels only (36 L1, 23 L2, 11 L3). vs.\nL294: eager (all) excludes 12 problems (5 correctness-failure, 7 no-kernels-generated) with no logged timing at all.\nL295: Compile’s own margin over eager (the ratio of “vs eager” to “vs compile” geomeans) is roughly 1.23–1.56$\\times$ here, meaningfully positive but well below the 2.27$\\times$ literature figure for full macro-architectures (cite72†Ansel et al., 2024 ): KernelBench L1/L2 problems are individual operators or short fusion chains, where dispatch/launch overhead consumes a larger fraction of wall time and Inductor has less surface area to find gains than in full pipelines.\nL296: We expect this margin to grow toward the literature figure for larger real-world models.\nL297: ### 6.3 Fallback Analysis\nL298: Panel D of Table cite83†1 classifies all 85 fallback kernels by root cause (name-based heuristic; full breakdown and examples in Appendix cite55†F ). Library dominance accounts for 64/85 (75%): 35 GEMM-dominant and 26 Conv-dominant cases where cuBLAS or cuDNN already operate near peak throughput, so optimizing a thin Triton epilogue only adds dispatch overhead (caught by Gate 4).\nL299: Performance-gate rejections: 15 L1 cases show a real per-kernel NCU improvement (up to $6.21\\times$ on one example) that Gate 4 still correctly rejects once dispatch overhead is included E2E. Seven L3 models (nn.LSTM/nn.GRU) generate zero Triton kernels.\nL300: Five fallbacks are terminal correctness failures (a $\\rho$-threshold rejection, two crashes, a timeout, and an output-shape mismatch), while a further 166 Gate-3 rejections across 46 problems are transient – recovered within a retry loop without failing the overall model (§cite21†4.4 , Appendix cite67†I ).\nL301: ### 6.4 The Multi-Kernel Composition Gap\nL302: Of the 250 problems, 126 are multi-kernel. Comparing each problem’s per-kernel speedups (from the search’s own “multi-kernel results” summary) against its final E2E verdict, 37 of these 126 (29%) show at least one individually-optimized sub-kernel ($>1.03\\times$) whose gain does not survive re-stitching: the E2E model is matched (21 cases) or regressed (16 cases). Two cases are unambiguous – every listed sub-kernel is individually optimized, yet the whole model is not (e.g.\nL303: a single $30.75\\times$ sub-kernel yields only $0.994\\times$ E2E) – and one L3 model has 4 of 6 sub-kernels individually sped up ($10.02\\times$ down to $1.16\\times$) yet still measures $0.93\\times$ E2E. Full case listing in Appendix cite57†F.2 .\nL304: This composition gap arises from (1) dispatch/re-stitching overhead, (2) implicit .contiguous() copies from memory-layout transitions, and (3) Amdahl’s law when the optimized kernel is off the critical path. It directly motivates Gate 4: relying on sub-kernel metrics alone would silently ship 16 E2E regressions in this dataset.\n"
